# `m[c]` is written twice in the inner loop's text and neither `m` nor `c` changes there, yet each turn read the row anew and asked it what it is: 140 instructions an element.
# The head's first operation is that read, so the row is read once in front of the loop, behind a test that the list holds it itself (a hole goes by the loop as written),
# and the loop is built once for each kind of row. What is known of the row ends where a turn writes elements or runs other code.
# f1 = total
# RUN: walk f1:Body mechanisms=all
# CHECK-LABEL: code f1:Body
# CHECK: = GetTypedArrayElement
# RUN(SHELL): run-in-shell globalThis.sink = print;
